  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Most Kenyans carry an English or Christian first name (Brian, Faith, Kevin, Mercy) or a Muslim one (Hassan, Amina), then a name from their own community, which often works as the family name: Kamau, Otieno, Wanjiru. Kikuyu children are traditionally named after their grandparents, so Wanjiru and Kamau recur in every family. Luo names tell the time or the conditions of birth: Otieno and Atieno at night, Odhiambo and Adhiambo in the evening, Ochieng and Achieng when the sun was out, Omondi and Amondi at dawn; names starting with O are for boys and with A for girls. Kalenjin names do the same with Kip for boys and Che (Chep) for girls: Kiprop and Cheptoo were born in the rain. People are usually called by either name.",
